article.overview:
An inflatable structure demonstrator is a test article built from fabric or multilayer "softgoods" that is packed compactly for launch and then expanded after delivery. Its purpose is to check whether inflatable structures can serve as lunar habitats, airlocks or other infrastructure. The most useful public examples are ground and field demonstrators rather than flown lunar hardware:

- a 12-foot (3.65 m) diameter concept demonstrator with an inflatable airlock, built by ILC Dover for NASA and tested at NASA Langley Research Center in 2007 [1];
- a 13-month field test habitat at McMurdo Station, Antarctica, from January 2008 to February 2009 [2][5];
- Lockheed Martin's softgoods habitat programme, which reported subscale burst tests in 2024 and plans an in-space demonstration around the end of the decade [3][4].
